},"quantitative_form":"L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*0.1) with lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+sqrt(1-lambda_1^2))/(lambda_1*(1+sqrt(1-lambda_2^2)))","readout_names":["lyapunov_exponent"],"statement":"In the non-Hermitian regime with eta=0.1, the Lyapunov exponent of the PUAMO is L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*0.1), where lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2')). The positive versus zero output of this formula identifies localization versus delocalization across the full parameter square.","statistical_basis":"The Lyapunov exponent is a theoretical quantity; the source reports the formula and uses it to characterize the localization transition. The experimental phase diagram is measured via the second moment <x^2> at t=6.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"}],"controls":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":95,"line_start":95,"support":"0<=lambda_1<=1 is called the coupling constant, as it controls the strength of coupling between neighboring lattice sites"},{"line_end":82,"line_start":82,"support":"lambda_1=0.67, 0.435, 0.2 in the Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":117,"line_start":117,"support":"lambda_1=0.5 and 0.25 in the non-Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":134,"line_start":134,"support":"we experimentally map the relationship between sigma and lambda_1, lambda_2 by scanning the whole parameter space"}],"definition":"Coupling constant controlling the strength of coupling between neighboring lattice sites in the shift operator S_lambda1_eta.","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"The source reports lambda_1 values 0.67, 0.435, 0.2 in the Hermitian phase diagram and 0.5, 0.25 in the non-Hermitian experiments; the conservative interval [0.2, 0.67] lies within the source's 0-to-1 coupling range and is covered by the reported full-parameter-space phase scans.","maximum":0.67,"minimum":0.2,"name":"lambda_1","unit":"dimensionless"},{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":104,"line_start":104,"support":"0<=lambda_2<=1 controls the coupling of the shift in the synthetic dimension"},{"line_end":82,"line_start":82,"support":"lambda_2=0.2, 0.435, 0.67 in the Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":117,"line_start":117,"support":"lambda_2=0.25 and 0.5 in the non-Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":134,"line_start":134,"support":"we experimentally map the relationship between sigma and lambda_1, lambda_2 by scanning the whole parameter space"}],"definition":"Coupling constant controlling the coupling of the shift in the synthetic dimension in the coin operator Q_lambda2_theta.","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"The source reports lambda_2 values 0.2, 0.435, 0.67 in the Hermitian phase diagram and 0.25, 0.5 in the non-Hermitian experiments; the conservative interval [0.2, 0.67] lies within the source's 0-to-1 coupling range and is covered by the reported full-parameter-space phase scans.","maximum":0.67,"minimum":0.2,"name":"lambda_2","unit":"dimensionless"}],"fixed_conditions":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":107,"line_start":107,"support":"we focus exclusively on irrational fields, specifically Phi=(sqrt(5)-1)/2"}],"condition_id":"irrational_field_phi","statement":"The quasiperiodic field Phi is set to the irrational value (sqrt(5)-1)/2."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":82,"line_start":82,"support":"For all plots, we set theta=0"},{"line_end":425,"line_start":425,"support":"We set theta=0"}],"condition_id":"theta_zero","statement":"The phase theta is set to 0 in the experiments."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":96,"line_start":96,"support":"For eta=0, the shift is balanced and, in particular, unitary."},{"line_end":107,"line_start":107,"support":"In the Hermitian setting eta=0, the model is called UAMO"}],"condition_id":"eta_zero","statement":"The non-Hermitian parameter eta is set to 0, corresponding to the Hermitian regime."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":425,"line_start":425,"support":"We set theta=0, eta=0.1"}],"condition_id":"eta_point_one","statement":"The non-Hermitian parameter eta is set to 0.1 in the experimental phase diagram."}],"mechanisms":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":111,"line_start":111,"support":"lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2'))"},{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"}],"equations":["lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2'))","L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|)"],"mechanism_id":"lambda0_structure","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":95,"line_start":95,"support":"lambda_1' = sqrt(1-lambda_1^2)"},{"line_end":104,"line_start":104,"support":"lambda_2' = sqrt(1-lambda_2^2)"},{"line_end":111,"line_start":111,"support":"lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2'))"}],"name":"one","role":"The integer one appearing in the radicands 1-lambda_1^2 and 1-lambda_2^2, and in the factors 1+lambda_1' and 1+lambda_2' of lambda_0.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":1.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"}],"name":"zero","role":"The integer zero used in the max function and in the Hermitian regime eta=0.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"}],"name":"two_pi","role":"The constant 2*pi appearing in the Lyapunov exponent formula.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":6.283185307179586},{"anchors":[{"line_end":95,"line_start":95,"support":"lambda_1' = sqrt(1-lambda_1^2)"},{"line_end":104,"line_start":104,"support":"lambda_2' = sqrt(1-lambda_2^2)"}],"name":"square_exponent","role":"The exponent 2 in the definitions lambda_1' = sqrt(1-lambda_1^2) and lambda_2' = sqrt(1-lambda_2^2).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":2.0},{"anchors":[{"line_end":95,"line_start":95,"support":"lambda_1' = sqrt(1-lambda_1^2)"},{"line_end":104,"line_start":104,"support":"lambda_2' = sqrt(1-lambda_2^2)"}],"name":"sqrt_exponent","role":"The exponent 1/2 in the definitions lambda_1' = sqrt(1-lambda_1^2) and lambda_2' = sqrt(1-lambda_2^2).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.5}],"statement":"The Lyapunov exponent of the PUAMO is given by L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|), where lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2')) with lambda_1' = sqrt(1-lambda_1^2) and lambda_2' = sqrt(1-lambda_2^2). This mechanism defines the structural constants used in the Lyapunov claims.","variable_names":["lambda_1","lambda_2","lambda_1'","lambda_2'","lambda_0","L","eta"]},{"anchors":[{"line_end":425,"line_start":425,"support":"We set theta=0, eta=0.1"},{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"}],"equations":["L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*0.1)"],"mechanism_id":"lyapunov_eta_point_one","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":425,"line_start":425,"support":"We set theta=0, eta=0.1"}],"name":"eta_point_one","role":"The non-Hermitian parameter eta set to 0.1 in the experimental phase diagram.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.1}],"statement":"For the non-Hermitian regime with eta=0.1, the Lyapunov exponent is given by L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*0.1), where lambda_0 is as defined in the lambda0_structure mechanism. The value 0.1 is the non-Hermitian parameter used in the experimental phase diagram.","variable_names":["L","lambda_0","eta"]}],"paper_scope":"The paper reports the first experimental realization of the unitary almost-Mathieu operator (UAMO) and its non-Hermitian extension (PUAMO) using single-photon quantum walks. It observes metal-insulator transitions in the Hermitian limit, PT-symmetry-breaking transitions, and a novel spectral transition where all quasienergies become purely imaginary. The selected quantitative cluster focuses on the Lyapunov exponent L_lambda1_lambda2_eta, which quantifies the inverse localization length and is given by L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|) with lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2')). Two claims are built for the private regimes eta=0 and eta=0.1, sharing public controls lambda_1 and lambda_2 in [0.2,0.67] and the common readout lyapunov_exponent.","readouts":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":218,"line_start":218,"support":"The significance of the Lyapunov exponent L is that it quantifies the inverse localization length of solutions to the secular equation"},{"line_end":108,"line_start":108,"support":"This can be inferred from the Lyapunov exponent L_lambda1_lambda2_Phi of the model, which describes the average decay of eigenstates"},{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"}],"definition":"The Lyapunov exponent L_lambda1_lambda2_eta of the PUAMO, which quantifies the inverse localization length of eigenstates. In the localized phase it is given by L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|).","name":"lyapunov_exponent","resolution":null,"statistic":"The Lyapunov exponent is a theoretical quantity derived from the transfer matrix; the source reports the formula and uses it to characterize the localization transition.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless"}],"source":{"arxiv_versioned_id":"2508.08255v1","citation":"Quan Lin, Observation of Metal-Insulator and Spectral Phase Transitions in Aubry-André-Harper Models, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/tz2n-lqxx","journal_doi":"10.1103/tz2n-lqxx"},"source_ambiguities":[]}
